  "geometryBasis": "Nine individually named OpenStreetMap footprints and seven retaining traces control the surviving stone and timber compound. The official public East Gardens plan confirms the named buildings and their ordering.",
  "sectionBasis": "The Imperial Household Agency dates and measures the Fujimi towers, the ward publishes the Tenshudai dimensions, and the exact Qualla public-domain photos document the stones and wood. Two photographer-dedicated originals show the Fujimi turret and Hyakunin guardhouse.",
  "limitations": [
    "The scope is the standing public East Gardens remains. The giant timber tenshu that burned in 1657, the later imperial residence, museum interiors and modern outbuildings are outside the represented scope.",
    "The nine named footprints are mapped. Roof spacing, unsurveyed facing heights, fine tile patterns and the surviving wall batters and courses are approximate.",
    "The original East Gardens terrain is retained. The nearby moat surface and broader landscaping are supplied by Earth context, and original coarse terrain cannot resolve every masonry step."
